Hannah’s flat is filled with boxes. Some rental she’d found close to the girl’s school a few weeks after she’d moved out of their place in Woburn. She really would miss that house; the treelined streets, the classic red brick. It was littered with memories of her girl's early years; she was bereft at leaving it. The future was more important than memories of an old house; a new family lived there now anyway. She could never go back. And besides, Hannah still had her daughters and a new home in Maranello they could make new memories in. Jonas had taken a job in Zurich; technically a slight promotion at his current firm.  Only an hour's plane ride away, too. Not much longer than the train ride from London to Milton Keynes. He still voiced his opinion that she was making a mistake moving to Ferrari, but it had stopped bothering her so much. She’d been grateful for the gardening leave; trying to move her life to a new country while trying to work too would have been impossible. Especially as she was expected to hit the ground running in February. It had felt an age away in August, so used to the constant struggle for points, the quiet months had gone deceptively quickly. She’d almost forgotten how good it was to start a book and finish it in the same week. She’d even managed a fairly impressive (by her standards) one hundred and twenty-nine-day streak on Duo Lingo too. Even if Laura still giggled whenever she’d try to test out her new Italian in flirty texts.
